    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 152030\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n130 PM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated thunderstorms will be possible across Mohave County\n  Wednesday afternoon and evening, with gusty winds and localized heavy\n  rainfall possible. \n\n* Elsewhere, breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts will continue \n  through the week with dry conditions and near-normal \n  temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nDespite respectable northward moisture advection this afternoon,\nwarm mid-level temperatures are keeping the region capped and\nstable with some just some scattered cumulus fields across our\neastern zones. Surface dewpoints have climbed into the low to mid\n50s across southern Nevada and northwest Arizona, and even into\nthe mid-60s along the Colorado River Valley. As a closed low drops\ninto Northern California and the Great Basin on Wednesday,\nnorthward moisture transport will continue and a few storms may \nfire up across Mohave County during the afternoon and evening \nhours as favorable jet dynamics help overcome the persistent \ncapping. Storms should tend to move swiftly thanks to elevated \nwind shear in place, but training storms will have the potential \nfor localized flooding, and the enhanced shear could result in a \nfew stronger storms capable of small hail and strong wind gusts. \n\nElsewhere, dry conditions are expected to continue as dry\nsouthwesterly flow keeps monsoonal moisture focused further east\ninto Arizona. Daily gusty winds can be expected with afternoon\ngusts of 25 to 35 mph expected through Wednesday evening, trending\nslightly weaker Thursday. The flow pattern will flatten out over\nthe weekend with wind speeds easing area wide and dry and\nseasonable weather continuing. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nDaily summertime wind pattern expected through the TAF period with \ngusty S-SW afternoon winds diminishing after sunset. So far today, \nstronger SE (140-160 deg) winds 10-15kt with gusts 20-25kt have \nalready set up. Expect a shift to the S-SW (180-220 deg) in the 19-\n20z timeframe with speeds and gusts continuing to increase into the \nafternoon. Isolated gusts may reach 25-30kt before sunset. Expect \nwinds to diminish after 03z, remaining southerly at or below 8kt. \nSimilar pattern expected again on Wednesday with SE-SSE winds \nincreasing around 16z, switching to the S-SW around 18-20z. VFR \nconditions prevail with no operationally significant cloud cover, \nand temperatures expected to top out around 100 degrees this \nafternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Daily wind patterns \nexpected at most sites through the TAF period. Strong SSE-S winds 10-\n15kt with gusts 20-25kt have already set up across the Mojave \nDesert. Expect winds to continue increasing into the afternoon hours \nwith an isolated gust near 30kt possible. KBIH remains light and \nvariable for the next few hours but expect a stronger SSE push after \n20-21z through around sunset, then lighter NW flow develops tonight. \nLight W winds at KDAG become light and VRB this afternoon, favoring \na NE direction before stronger WSW winds 10-15kt with higher gusts \narrive around 23-00z. KDAG should follow their usual daily wind \ntrend with stronger WSW-WNW winds in the evening, diminishing during \nthe overnight hours. Winds at KIFP and KEED should diminish somewhat \nafter sunset but confidence remains higher that elevated winds may \npersist overnight with isolated gusts 15-20kt overnight. Isolated \nthunderstorms are expected, mainly over northwestern Arizona and \neastern Lincoln County this afternoon and evening...not expecting \nimpacts from associated outflow at the terminals. Otherwise, VFR \nconditions prevail with no operationally significant cloud cover \nelsewhere.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
